# Caddy's bootstrap on a real box. Readme.md section 16.
#
# Still no site blocks, and for the same reason as `infra/local/Caddyfile`: an
# app's route is written by the worker over the admin API, from what the
# control plane's database says is live. A site block here would be a second
# answer to "where does traffic go", and the reconciler could not re-assert the
# first one without fighting it.
#
# The console is routed the same way (`worker/runtimes/proxy.py`'s console
# route) rather than being given a site block here, because two servers cannot
# share `:443` — and because it means a proxy that came up empty gets the route
# to the console restated by the same reconcile pass that restates the apps.
#
# What differs from local is only the two lines below.
#
# `auto_https` is left at its default, which is on. There are no site blocks to
# derive hostnames from, so nothing is certificated by *this* file: Caddy gets
# a certificate for an app when the worker writes that app's route, because the
# `host` matcher in the written route is what names the hostname and the server
# it is written onto listens on 443. That is why `VD_PUBLIC_SCHEME=https` has
# to be set before the first deploy rather than after.
#
# The admin API is on a **unix socket**, and that is the one line here that
# differs from local for a security reason rather than a port-number one.
#
# It cannot be a TCP port on this box. Caddy dials each app's login gate by
# container name, so Caddy must share a Docker network with every gate — and a
# gate shares its network namespace with the app container, i.e. with code we
# have never read. An admin port bound on that network hands any deployed app a
# rewrite of the entire route table: every app's traffic, the console's, and the
# hostnames certificates get issued for. `origins` is not a defence, because the
# Host header belongs to whoever sends it.
#
# A socket has no address on any network. It is mounted into the worker alone
# (the `caddy_admin` volume in docker-compose.yml) — the only process that
# writes routes.
#
# `|0666` without apology: the mount is the access control, and the socket is in
# a volume no other service gets. The alternative is running Caddy as the
# worker's uid, which also means granting it CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for 80/443 and
# moving the certificate store's ownership — real complexity to buy a mode bit
# on a file nothing else can open.
#
# `origins localhost` because Caddy checks the Host header even over a socket,
# and `proxy.UNIX_AUTHORITY` sends `localhost`.

{
	admin unix//run/caddy-admin/admin.sock|0666 {
		origins localhost
	}

	# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. Not optional in practice: an
	# ACME account with no contact is how a renewal failure becomes an outage
	# nobody was told about.
	email {$VD_ACME_EMAIL}
}
